### Abstract
<!-- (RECOMMENDED) Provide the original abstract or an adjusted one. -->
Multi-material stacks of carbon fiber-reinforced polymers and metals are widely used in aerospace manufacturing. However, drilling through these stacks is challenging because the constituent materials exhibit substantially different material properties. This can lead to increased tool wear and reduced drill hole quality. Vibration-assisted drilling has been proposed as a method to improve chip evacuation and reduce process temperatures, thereby mitigating tool wear.

In this study tool condition monitoring in vibration-assisted drilling of multi-material stacks using a gated recurrent unit network was investigated. To support industrial integration without additional sensor hardware, the model uses only current signals of the spindle bearings. Its transferability was evaluated across different spindles and material combinations. In addition, physics-informed monotonicity constraints were introduced to enforce a non-decreasing wear progression during model training and to improve robustness under changed production conditions.

The results show that tool condition monitoring for vibration-assisted drilling of multi-material stacks is possible, even under changing production conditions. Adding a monotonicity constraint improved model performance under such changed conditions, indicating its potential to increase the robustness of current-based tool condition monitoring across production domains.

